ROADMAP.md:458:| R-0575 | The per-function facts report and query from slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-facts-report), 2026-11-06) | every function of each slice has a report derived from the Lean rules, with a versioned schema: capabilities and the grants and resources actually reachable, allocation and allocator, trusted, foreign and opaque boundaries with their assumptions, each operation that could fail and how it is handled, termination (evidence with its named premises, or the responsible source permission and the call path to it), recursion depth and stack bound or why none is established, proof status per obligation, and completeness, each with its reason, evidence and source location; a permission that justifies nothing is reported as a fact, which a `Concrete.toml` policy may refuse (the decided refusals of an unmatched `#[proof]` and a duplicate `#[may_not_terminate]` stay) (carried from slice 0 into CheckedCore and the schema, spec obligations O1 and O4); the schema already has fields for what memory a function writes (frames) and what its outputs depend on (dependency flow), reported "not established" until derived: definite, maybe and never writes per `&mut` place and the output-to-input dependencies (control dependence, cleanup and opaque calls included) are derived into these fields as soon as `&mut` lands, not in E–G, while R-0524, R-0473 and R-0383 keep source frames, contracts and package summaries; facts are preserved across indirect calls, specialization and package boundaries (R-0588); from slice 0 the compiler answers a query about one function or module from the same facts, as readable text and in the machine format (the command is `concrete facts`); the report never claims what the rules do not establish; every fact is a typed judgment in Lean with a derivation-soundness theorem (and obligation-discovery completeness where it claims absence), one derivation feeds the readable view, the machine format and every policy, each fact has a closed status (established, conditional, refuted, not established, not applicable) with per-fact coverage, unknown never satisfies a policy, and answers carry typed identities, versioned schema and semantics and artifact digests; from slice 0 the report also carries the build identity (compiler SHA, toolchain pins and target), each program's maximum stack use, measured from the final binary on both backends, and the trusted-base statement naming the Lean kernel, LLVM printer and LLVM, LCB's MIR→assembly step, linker, OS and named runtime premises as not proven ([early decisions](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, with golden tests; a planted wrong fact and a planted unknown version are caught; Home: spec (the stack-bound claim and, from slice 0, the refusal of an unknown or over-budget bound against the `Concrete.toml` stack budget) and tooling (the report). Gate: stack, the final-artifact bound checked against the claim and the budget ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0499 |
ROADMAP.md:466:| R-0589 | Declared assumptions in `Concrete.toml`, with a change-detecting check ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-concrete-toml), 2026-11-06) | a project declares its target, heap, stack, foreign-function and trusted-surface assumptions in its `Concrete.toml` only where the facts cannot derive them; the section is typed, versioned data and an unknown key or version is refused; the facts query reports each assumption with the facts that rely on it; any change to the declared set is reported as an assumption diff and fails CI until the diff is recorded with the change; the sections are the typed, versioned `[policy]` and `[assumptions]` tables | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:468:| R-0576 | The predictable policy ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-predictable-policy), 2026-11-06) | an optional module or project policy, built on the per-function facts (R-0575), refuses at compile time any function in its scope that allocates, blocks or calls foreign code, and, when the policy demands it, any function without termination evidence or a stack bound; admission requires established evidence of no allocation, no blocking and no foreign calls, so an unknown or incomplete fact is refused; the policy explicitly states whether named trusted assumptions are acceptable (Q13); a violation names the function, the fact and its source; the query reports which policy covers each function; a planted violation and a planted unknown fact are both refused; the policy is declared in the project's `Concrete.toml` ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-concrete-toml)) as typed, versioned data, a module may tighten it but never relax it, and dependencies are checked against the consumer's policy transitively and fail-closed; Home: tooling (the optional `Concrete.toml` policy over the facts; the mandatory slice-0 stack-budget refusal is R-0575's). Gate: the policy's refusal controls ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:504:| R-0596 | `Secret<T>` with its noninterference and constant-time proofs, from slice 7 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06)) | at slice 7, scalar `Secret<T>`, `classify` (of a value passed in), `declassify(x, "reason")`, `ct_select` and secret comparisons land; secret control flow, secret indices and secret lengths are refused; scalar secrets are `Copy`; under the randomness decision, the entropy capability arrives at slice 6, its output is `Secret<T>` at slice 7 and fill-bytes arrives at slice 15, with its source a named trusted premise in facts ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness)); later increments add `ct_eq` on arrays and views and linear, wiped secret arrays at slice 15, wiped heap secrets at slice 19, and grant-backed sources at slices 16, 18 and 27; noninterference and constant time are proved for the type system under the baseline observer model; each `declassify` is traced in the facts; the legacy `constant_time_tag` is rewritten with `ct_eq`; the LCB plan gains constant-time and wipe preservation, and CI checks the binary constant-time premise; Home: spec (the entropy capability and its trusted premise). Gate: the slice's own acceptance gates ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0592, R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:542:| R-0259 | Runtime-safety obligations: casts, loops, stack, panics, byte, text and path boundaries | the safety corpus is discharged or reported | R-0495 |
