docs/decisions.md:1252:## Owner decisions
docs/decisions.md:1254:**Only the project owner decides these items, OD1 – OD157.** All are answered. OD1 – OD32 (exit 2n, closed) and OD47 were decided on 2026-10-29 (OD23 – OD32 by the strictness pass of that day). OD33 – OD157 were decided on 2026-10-31, by the owner's rule that an answer every reviewer agreed on and as strict as Austral or stricter is taken, and on 2026-11-01, when the owner approved the final set for the rest; the authority decision of the same day supersedes OD38's and OD156's `Root` readings (OD94 was withdrawn: MOD-1 already refuses its case, bug 337). The answers are recorded in [docs/decisions.md](decisions.md), state the decided rules in chapters now (owner revision, 2026-11-03), then become Lean in phases B–D, and are enforced as each new-compiler slice lands. The questions raised while recording them were answered on 2026-11-02, the owner deciding the first four and, by the owner's rule of that day, Claude and Codex agreeing on the best long-term answer for the rest ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02)); they revise OD24, OD38, OD43, OD83, OD102, OD146 and OD156 and the authority rules. The spec audit's questions were answered the same day ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)), revising OD140 and STMT-13. Traits are exported and imported like any other declaration (owner, 2026-11-02). Core was provisionally approved on 2026-11-05 ([record](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); A2b formalization is current, and the A2c freeze awaits the four design theorems, the A2i integration stage (2026-11-06) and an outside-reader pass. New questions follow the same rule: No agent, orchestrator or
docs/decisions.md:1298:| OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
docs/decisions.md:1440:| OD150 | <a id="od150"></a>Which text governs the grammar until it is resynchronized (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether, until OPEN-77's resynchronization, `grammar/concrete.ebnf` or the rule entries govern where they disagree. Bears on SYN-1 and OPEN-77. The current compiler: the parser accepts forms the EBNF does not derive (`import a.{f};`, `m::f()`, `f::<T>(x)`, value `if` and `match`, `pub` fields, bodyless declarations) and the EBNF keeps forms the entries removed (`E#V`, the C-style `for`, `break <expr>`); SYN-1 calls the EBNF the grammar, and the LL(1) checkers read it. Options: (a) the rule entries govern, and the EBNF is descriptive until the resync; (b) the EBNF governs, and an entry that disagrees is pending; (c) neither: each disagreement is decided one by one under OPEN-77. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-150 | see OPEN-150 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Rule entries govern |
docs/decisions.md:1441:| OD151 | <a id="od151"></a>Module paths of more than one level (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether a qualified path may name a member of a module nested more than one level deep (`a::b::f()`). Bears on SYN-10, MOD-4 and OPEN-139. The current compiler: the parser's lowercase module-path branch accepts one qualification pair: `a::b::f()`, with `f` in `mod b` inside `mod a`, is a parse error on both consumers (E0001, `expected <, got ident(f)`), because after `a::b` it accepts only `::<` (explicit type arguments). SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 gives `std::fs::read_to_string` as an example of module qualification, which this parser does not accept. Options: (a) one qualification pair, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 says so; (b) any depth, every segment before the last naming a module (a repetition, which stays LL(1)). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-151 | see OPEN-151 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allow multi-level module paths `a::b::f` |
docs/decisions.md:1442:| OD152 | <a id="od152"></a>Whether a path's first letter decides its syntactic form (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether the first letter of a path's first segment decides the syntactic form of `X::y`, or one path form leaves to name resolution whether `X` is a type or a module. Bears on SYN-10, SYN-1 and MOD-1. The current compiler: the parser chooses by the first letter of `X`: an uppercase `X` gives an associated-function call or an enum literal, and a lowercase `X` the flattened name `X_y`, which name resolution looks up as one identifier (bug 343); a module named with an uppercase letter goes through the associated-function form, and `M::f()` on `mod M` builds and runs on both consumers. SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 presents the case split as how the parse stays LL(1). Options: (a) the first letter decides the syntactic form, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 states it; (b) the parser reads `X::y` as one path form, which is also LL(1), and name resolution decides whether `X` is a type or a module. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-152 | see OPEN-152 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Resolve `X::y` by declaration, not by the first letter |
docs/decisions.md:1443:| OD153 | <a id="od153"></a>Program functions named like a builtin (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether a program may declare a function whose name is a builtin's, other than `print`, `println` and `append` (OPEN-133) and the names MOD-3 reserves, and which function the program's calls of that name reach. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-1, MOD-8 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: a program function `string_length` is called by the program's calls on both consumers, and so are program functions named `string_len` and `String_len` (`string_len` returns 7 and `String_len` returns 9; a program that calls both exits 79 on both consumers), while `destroy`, `discard`, `abort`, `alloc`, `free`, `alloc_array`, `free_array` and `realloc_array` are reserved (E0578). The calls the compiler itself generates are bound by identity (MOD-8), so only the program's own calls change. Options: (a) reserve every remaining builtin's name, aliases included, and MOD-3 lists them (`print`, `println` and `append` stay with OPEN-133); (b) allow the declaration, and the program's own calls reach it, as the current compiler does, and MOD-3 says so; (c) allow the declaration, and a program reaches the builtin by a qualified name. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-153 | see OPEN-153 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse program functions named like builtins |
docs/decisions.md:1444:| OD154 | <a id="od154"></a>Whether the string builtins require `Alloc` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `string_concat`, `string_slice`, `string_substr`, `string_trim`, `int_to_string`, `bool_to_string`, `float_to_string`, `string_push_char`, `string_append`, `string_append_int` and `string_append_bool` require the `Alloc` capability. Bears on MEM-7, TRUST-4 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: Check gives none of these builtins a capability, and a function with no capabilities calls `string_concat` and `string_append_int` and exits 42 on both consumers (the other names were not probed). MEM-7@1 lists `string_reserve` and `append` among the builtins that require `Alloc`; `append` is accepted without `Alloc` (bug 349), and it expands into `string_append`, `string_append_int`, `string_append_bool` and `string_push_char`. MEM-7's rationale says allocation is an effect a signature shows. Options: (a) each builtin named above requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists it; (b) a stated subset of them requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists that subset; (c) only the builtins MEM-7 already lists require `Alloc`, so `string_reserve` and `append` do and the names above do not, and MEM-7 says so; (d) no string builtin requires `Alloc`, `string_reserve` and `append` included, and MEM-7 drops those two. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-154 | see OPEN-154 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allocating string builtins need `Alloc` (MEM-7; closes bug 349) |
docs/decisions.md:1445:| OD155 | <a id="od155"></a>`unwrap` beside TYPE-24's conversions (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `unwrap` stays an intrinsic beside the conversions TYPE-24 lists (`n.0` and `n as T`), becomes one of those conversions, or is removed. Bears on TYPE-24, TYPE-11, TYPE-10 and OPEN-54. The current compiler: Check gives `unwrap(m)` the newtype's inner type (E0270 when the argument is not a newtype); `return unwrap(m);` exits 42 on both consumers, and `let x: Int = unwrap(m);` followed by `if x == 42` fails the native build at SSA verification (E0715, bug 345). TYPE-24 lists `n.0` and `n as T` and does not list `unwrap`. OPEN-54 decides this only if its answer removes newtypes; while newtypes stay, this question stays. Options: (a) keep `unwrap` as an intrinsic and leave it off TYPE-24's list, as the current compiler and TYPE-24 do; (b) `unwrap(n)` is a conversion, TYPE-24 lists it, and TYPE-11 restricts it as it restricts `n.0`; (c) remove `unwrap`, leaving `n.0` and `n as T`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-155 | see OPEN-155 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No builtin `unwrap`; use `match` |
docs/decisions.md:1446:| OD156 | <a id="od156"></a>The capability of `get_args` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Which capability the builtin `get_args` requires, or whether the builtin is removed. Bears on TRUST-4, STMT-7, TRUST-9 and OPEN-136. The current compiler: Check gives `get_args()` the result `HeapArray<String>` and requires `Process` (E0520 with `Env` only); the builtin has no implementation on either consumer (bug 347). STMT-7's rationale says command-line arguments come from `std.args` with `Env`, and OD8 gives `main` the root resource value (TRUST-9). Options: (a) `Env`, as `std.args` requires; (b) `Process`, as the current compiler's signature says; (c) another capability, or none; (d) remove the builtin, and command-line arguments come from `std.args`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-156 | see OPEN-156 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: `get_args` requires `with(Env)`, and `with(Alloc)` when it allocates. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: it also takes the `Environment` grant, and an allocator when it allocates ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-filesystem)) |
docs/decisions.md:1447:| OD157 | <a id="od157"></a>How the type `Unit` is written (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether the type `Unit`, which STMT-7 names as a result type of `main` and which other rules and the builtins catalog also name, has a spelling in type positions, and which spelling. Bears on STMT-7, TYPE-4, SYN-1 and OPEN-66. The current compiler: a function with no result type returns no value, and `fn main() { return; }` exits 0; `fn main() -> Unit` and `let x: Unit = f();` are refused at resolve (E0108, unknown type 'Unit'); `fn main() -> ()` is a parse error (E0001, `expected type`). STMT-7@3 says `main`'s result type is `u8` or `Unit`. OPEN-66 concerns the unit value's literal; this entry concerns the type in type positions, and the two cross-reference each other. Options: (a) no spelling: the type is written only by omitting the result type, and STMT-7 says so; (b) `Unit` is a builtin type name, accepted wherever a type is; (c) `()` is the type's spelling; (d) both `Unit` and `()` are accepted. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-157 | see OPEN-157 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): The type is written `Unit`, its value `()` |
